This Week's Macroeconomic Outlook: CPI Data Approaches, Potentially Further Confirming the Rationality of the Federal Reserve's Rate Cut Cycle

On December 13, despite the Federal Reserve cutting rates as expected this week and releasing a more dovish signal than anticipated, the real challenges faced in the field of artificial intelligence have led to a complex divergence in the performance of the U.S. stock and bond markets. This week, long-term U.S. Treasury yields rose, with the 10-year Treasury yield increasing by about 5 basis points during the 'Federal Reserve Rate Cut Week.' The macroeconomic outlook for this week is as follows:

Monday 22:30, Federal Reserve Governor Mylan will give a speech;
Monday 23:30, FOMC permanent voter and New York Fed President Williams will discuss the economic outlook;
Thursday 01:30, 2027 FOMC voter and Atlanta Fed President Bostic will talk about the economic outlook;
Thursday 21:30, U.S. November unadjusted CPI year-on-year/core CPI year-on-year, U.S. November seasonally adjusted CPI month-on-month/core CPI month-on-month;
Thursday 21:30, U.S. initial jobless claims for the week ending December 13;
Friday 23:00, U.S. December University of Michigan Consumer Sentiment Index final value, U.S. December one-year inflation expectations final value.

The release of U.S. CPI data this week will become a key turning point for the dollar's trend. If the CPI data is below expectations (the latest data being 3%, still above the Federal Reserve's target of 2%), it will further confirm the rationality of the Federal Reserve's rate cut cycle, and the dollar may face further downward pressure; conversely, it could reverse this trend. #美联储降息 #btc #ETH
